  "query_preview": "..Another pleasant weather day underway across the \nregion today. Much like Friday, temperatures are running around \n5-10 degrees below seasonal normal across south to southeast Utah,\nand near normal elsewhere. Water vapor and visible satellite show\nmoisture/cloud cover lifting into southern Utah around a cutoff \nlow, but dry conditions are expected to be maintained throughout \nthe daytime hours Saturday.\n\nThe cutoff low and associated moisture will gradually get tugged \nnortheastward into the northern jet. Low end (~10-30%) shower \nchances then creep into portions of southern Utah overnight into \nSunday, with a few isolated thunderstorms also possible Sunday \nafternoon. While instability is limited, PWAT anomalies to around \n200% of normal will support at least some localized threat of a \ncouple cells with more moderate rain rates. While widespread flash\nflooding is not anticipated, have introduced \"Possible\" ratings \nin the Flash Flood Potential to Bryce Canyon, Capitol Reef, the \nGrand Staircase, and Zion, and primarily for the more rain \nsensitive basins in those areas. Similar conditions are \nanticipated Monday as the low further ejects into the northern \nstream.\n\nTuesday onward will see the deepening northern stream cutoff \nanother broad low across the western CONUS before gradually \ntranslating eastward through the Great Basin and Intermountain \nWest. Ensemble members still show some differences in how exactly \nthis transpires, with some members now showing some potential for \na brief dry slot Wednesday. Broadly speaking though, ample \nmoisture advection into the area in combination with strong \nsynoptic forcing will support increased chances of widespread \nprecipitation Tuesday through Friday (excluding Wednesday if the \ndry slot develops). Chances likely will be maximized in the \nThursday/Friday time frames as ensembles favor that window for \nwhen the trough axis passes through the local forecast region. \nThis will also be the window where a slightly cooler airmass \nshifts in, and snow levels initially above 10000 ft drop to around\n8000-8500 ft and allow a brief window of modest high elevation \nsnow accumulation. Forecast water amounts once again have not \nchanged much with many areas expected to see 0.50-1.50\" or so, and\nup to around 2.00\" in favored high terrain. Given limited cold \nair, forecast snow totals are around 3-8\" for elevations \ngenerally above 9500 ft.",
